f.formatException(record.exc_info)\nif record.exc_text:\n    if s[-1:] != \"\\n\": s = s + \"\\n\"\n    s = s + record.exc_text\nif record.stack_info:\n    if s[-1:] != \"\\n\": s = s + \"\\n\"\n    s = s + self.formatStack(record.stack_info)\nreturn s\n```\n\nSimplest: temporarily patch record.message via object.__setattr__? record is a LogRecord — setting record.message is normal attribute set on instance, doesn't touch msg/args. Actually `record.message` is a normal instance attribute; setting it is allowed and doesn't alter msg/args. So:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    record.message = record.getMessage()\n    if budget applies:\n        original = record.message\n        record.message = truncated\n    msg = super().format(record)  # recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() -- overwrites!\n```\n\nWait, super().format recomputes record.message = record.getMessage(), overwriting our truncated value. Hmm, in Python 3.11 Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage() only if not hasattr? Let's check: CPython 3.12:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    record.message = record.getMessage()\n    if self.usesTime():\n        record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n    s = self.formatMessage(record)\n    ...\n```\n\nYes, it overwrites. So instead, do our own formatting: compute truncated message, then monkeypatch? Alternative approach: use a proxy — copy the record? Copying avoids mutating shared record but then stack/multiline prefix uses record.message. We need formatTime high_precision (uses self.converter, record.created, record.msecs) — copying works fine.\n\nBut also `record.exc_text` caching — copying means exc_text not cached on original; acceptable. But careful: a copy means other handlers get original full record — good.\n\nHowever, subtlety: multiline prefix computation `prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]` uses record.message from copy. Fine.\n\nWhich is better: copy record via `copy.copy(record)` (stdlib copy) then set `.message`? But super().format will still recompute message = record.getMessage() on the copy → overwrites. So we must avoid super().format and replicate base behavior. Alternatively set copied record's msg to truncated and args to () — that changes copy's msg/args but not original. That's acceptable (\"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args\" — shared original). Simpler: use a copy and set msg=truncated, args=().\n\nHmm but copy of record retains exc_info, stack_info. Then super().format(record_copy) recomputes message = getMessage() = truncated. \n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n        msg = self._format_record(record)  # original code path\n    else:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        if len(message) > budget:\n            r = copy.copy(record)\n            r.msg = _truncate(message, budget)\n            r.args = ()\n            msg = self._format_record(r)  # uses r.message\n```\n\nBut `_format_record` needs record.message set for the prefix logic. In original code:\n\n```python\nmsg = super().format(record)\nif \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n    prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n    msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n```\n\nrecord.message is set by super().format. For the copy, record.message gets set to truncated message. Good.\n\nBut careful: `msg.find(record.message)` — for truncated message containing \"...<omitted>...\" fine. But also newlines: if truncated message contains newline, prefix computed from start of message in formatted string. Fine.\n\nAlso `getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True` — spec says exactly True. Using `is True` works. But note extra={\"keep_full_message\": True} sets attribute on record. Also careful about extra with key colliding... fine.\n\nBudget min 32, marker \"...<omitted>...\" length 15. head = ceil((budget-15)/2), tail = budget-15-head.\n\nTruncate: if len(message) <= budget: return message. else head+marker+tail. Note spec example: budget 32 → 9 head, 8 tail.\n\nValidator: bool not int → ValueError. `type(max_message_chars) is not int or max_message_chars < 32` → ValueError.\n\nAlso `_setup_logger` passes max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS.\n\nNote: `_setup_logger()` runs at import. NewLineFormatter positional args preserved.\n\nenviron: add\n\n```python\ndef _log_max_message_chars(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n    if v is None:\n        return None\n    value = int(v)  # may raise ValueError\n    if value < 32:\n        raise ValueError(f\"must be >= 32, got {value}\")\n    return value\n```\n\nError wrapping includes name: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: ...`. For empty string int(\"\") raises ValueError(\"invalid literal for int()...\") wrapped with name. Good. But TypeError branch in descriptor: `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")` — for ValueError single-arg fine.\n\nAdd descriptor: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(_log_max_message_chars)`.\n\nstats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})` for each m in msgs.\n\nLet me check for other places where _setup_logger called / tests. Also check logger imports (copy needed). Let me read logger.py lines 1-32 to confirm imports (already exposed ev-30754 starts at 33). Read lines 1-32.\n\nLet's do edits now. First read logger head for imports."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9968,"completion_tokens":1714,"total_tokens":11682,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1688},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":5872},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}